{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-sci":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-sci/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/lem-feynman-markov-identification","@type":["folio-assistant-sci:Lemma","doco:Section"],"label":"lem:feynman-markov-identification","kind":"lemma","title":"Markov-weighted eigenbasis sum equals the Markov trace","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/lem-feynman-eigenbasis-decomposition","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/thm-jones-markov-trace"],"proofs":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prf-feynman-markov-identification"],"tags":["markov-trace","wedderburn","feynman","hecke","identification"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.FeynmanPathIntegrals.markov_identification","text":"feynman-markov-identification.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"lem:feynman-markov-identification\"></a><p><em>Let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code> be real, not a root of unity, with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q^2 + 1 \\neq 0</code>, and let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{tr}_M</code> be a Markov trace on the Hecke tower at the parameter <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">z = q^3/(q^2+1)</code>. For <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">x \\in H_n(R, q)</code>, the transition amplitude on the eigenbasis is the Markov-weighted sum of partial traces:</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  \\langle\\psi_f|\\psi_i\\rangle_{\\mathrm{eigenbasis}}\n  :=\n  \\sum_{\\lambda \\vdash n}\n  w_\\lambda \\cdot \\mathrm{tr}_{S_\\lambda}(x),</code></pre>\n<p><em>where <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">w_\\lambda</code> is the Ocneanu weight of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda</code> at <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">z</code> and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{tr}_{S_\\lambda}</code> is the trace of the seminormal representation <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">S_\\lambda</code>. This amplitude equals <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{tr}_M(x)</code>. The weight <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">w_\\lambda</code> vanishes whenever <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\lambda</code> has three or more rows, so only the partitions with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\ell(\\lambda) \\leq 2</code> contribute, and for those</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  w_\\lambda = \\frac{\\dim_q(V_\\lambda)}{\\dim_q(V^{\\otimes n})}\n  = \\frac{[\\lambda_1 - \\lambda_2 + 1]_q}{[2]_q^{\\,n}}.</code></pre>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/mass-theory/feynman-markov-identification.md","heading":"Lemma","number":"13.13.2","page":"b/lem-feynman-markov-identification","qa":{"counts":{"pass":67,"fail":1,"warn":0,"na":52,"unknown":0,"stale":112},"milnor":{"total":3,"notPassing":0}},"lean":{"expected":true,"ref":"qou:QOU.StandardModel.FeynmanPathIntegrals.markov_identification_of_markovTrace","via":"ref","path":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/StandardModel/MarkovIdentificationInstance.lean","compiles":"unchecked","sorry":false,"sorryBasis":"text"}}
